.he "kill (1)                     9/22/26                     kill (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
kill
- send a signal to a process
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
kill [ -sig ] pid ...
.br
.bd
kill -h
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Kill sends a signal to each process named.  The signal is SIGTERM
(15) unless -sig names another, and may be given as a number or as a
name: -9 and -KILL are the same signal, and a leading SIG is optional,
so -HUP, -hup and -SIGHUP are the same word as well.
.sp
Only the super-user may signal a process belonging to another user,
and no process may signal itself.
.sp
With -h, kill lists the signals it can send, one to a line, and stops.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-sig		Send signal sig.  A number from 1 to 15, or one of the
		names below.
.sp
.ti -10
-h		List the signals and stop.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SIGNALS
.in +5
.br
.in +5
 1  HUP   hangup
.br
 2  INT   interrupt
.br
 3  QUIT  quit
.br
 4  ILL   illegal instruction
.br
 5  TRAP  trace trap
.br
 6  BACK  control-B typed
.br
 7  TINT  tty input record available
.br
 8  FPE   floating point exception
.br
 9  KILL  kill (cannot be caught or ignored)
.br
10  BUS   bus error
.br
11  SEGV  segmentation violation
.br
12  SYS   bad argument to system call
.br
13  PIPE  write on a pipe with no one to read it
.br
14  ALRM  alarm clock
.br
15  TERM  software termination signal
.sp
The two names that are not the familiar ones are the kernel's: 6 and 7
are the signals the terminal driver raises, and this system has no IOT
or EMT for them to mean.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The distribution shipped no kill, so this is not a port: the v6 and v7
sources are on the disks and the interface above is v7's, with the
names and -h added.  The system call is the one that was always here.
.sp
Signal 0 and signals of 16 and above are refused rather than sent.
The kernel does not range-check the signal.  It looks the signal up in
slist, the sixteen dispositions belonging to the process being
signalled, and slist is a field of that process's entry in the process
table rather than a table of its own: an index of 16 or more reads
past it, into the rest of the entry and the next one along, and what
it finds there is compared with "ignore" to decide whether the signal
is delivered at all.  If it is, the delivery indexes the same array
with the same number a second time - reading a disposition nobody set
and clearing it - so the process is either terminated on that number
or jumped to whatever address the word it found held.  signal (2),
which does set dispositions, range-checks and answers EINVAL, so
nothing out there was put there on purpose.
.sp
A process id of 0 is refused for the same kind of reason.  The system
call documents it as "all other processes with the same controlling
tty", and sends it to ALL of them: the shell that ran kill is one of
them, and it does not come back.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/bin/kill
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
